<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  PHASE_02_RECONSTRUCTED_POC — LEVEL 1 of the entitlement investigation:
	  REQUESTED_IN_REPO. This file is a record of what the PoC would ASK FOR.

	  It is NOT a granted capability, it is NOT evidence that JIT works, and it must
	  never be reported as "JIT enabled". The four levels this project keeps apart:
	     1 requested_in_repo      this file, a repository fact (readable anywhere);
	     2 embedded_at_build      decided by the signing step (see tools/build_ios.sh);
	     3 granted_to_signature   readable only off a built, signed product
	                              (tools/inspect_entitlements.sh);
	     4 observed_at_runtime    what the app measured: jit.map_jit_probe.

	  WIRING DECISION (pass 03, resolved and audited — tools/audit_entitlement_config.py):
	  this file is deliberately NOT referenced by CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS, in Debug or in
	  Release. Reasons, both measured or documented, not stylistic:

	   * On iOS-based platforms every entitlement must be allowlisted by the provisioning
	     profile. A free/personal-team or sideload profile cannot allowlist a JIT
	     entitlement, so attaching it cannot obtain anything.
	   * Attaching an entitlement the profile does not allow makes the signed build or the
	     installation fail ("provisioning profile does not include the ... entitlement";
	     device error 0xE8008016 "The entitlements specified ... do not match those
	     specified in your provisioning profile"). That would break the ONLY path that can
	     validate anything — the user's own install on the iPhone — in exchange for a
	     capability that is not obtainable there anyway (physical run #2 measured
	     MAP_JIT refused errno=1 on the device).

	  The wiring was investigated as a possible project error and is classified as a
	  DOCUMENTED CONFIGURATION DECISION, not a defect: the file's own history already said
	  it was intentional, and the evidence above makes the decision correct. What was
	  missing — and what pass 03 added — is the audit and the explicit opt-in below, so the
	  state can no longer be "a file nothing references".

	  OPT-IN (only for a signing context that really can carry it, e.g. a macOS runner
	  experiment with a profile that allows the entitlement; never for the phone):
	      xcodebuild ... CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS=RuntimePoC/WinlatorPhase02.entitlements
	  If it is ever wired into the project, it must be set for Debug AND Release together
	  (never for a single configuration: that produces two products with different
	  entitlement requests from one project) — the audit enforces exactly that.

	  The physical validation keeps signing however the device owner signs it and letting
	  the app REPORT what it got:
	      jit.map_jit_probe      PASS     -> MAP_JIT was granted
	      jit.map_jit_probe      BLOCKED  -> refused, with the real errno
	      jit.execution_allowed  PASS/BLOCKED
	  A refusal there is a finding about the signing context, not proof that the port is
	  broken.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
